5 #edb/ref 17592186517884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair in a ballet class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518116 #edb/ref 1074 "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518188 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518216 #edb/ref 1074 "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518283 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518283 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518283 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518310 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518216 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
